          "content": {
            "text": "Read file /Users/ylmacstudio/.config/opencode/skills/opencode-kaola-project-runner/SKILL.md, lines 1-166\n1: ---\n2: name: opencode-kaola-project-runner\n3: description: Use when the controlling Agent should communicate with an OpenCode main conversation through an exact owned ACP session by starting it, reading evidence, sending Agent-selected prompts or keys, reading replies, and stopping only that session.\n4: ---\n5: \n6: # OpenCode Kaola Project Runner\n7: \n8: This Skill is a communication driver for OpenCode. It gives the controlling Agent a\n9: measured ACP channel to one exact owned session; it does not choose commands, Workflow modes, cadence, state, approvals,\n10: retries, or completion policy. The separate Skill `kaola-project-runner` (display name Project\n11: Runner) is the main orchestrator when a host Agent is supervising workers; this Skill stays\n12: transport-only.\n13: \n14: ## Transport\n15: \n16: ACP is the only transport (Issue #130). The ACP command is `opencode acp`; prompts travel over its stdio JSON-RPC, never shell eval. Login is a human act outside the Runner and needs no terminal. This platform's ACP quirks are in [references/acp.md](references/acp.md) \u2014 open it when a quirk matters. A request for the retired PTY transport is refused with `transport-pty-retired` and changes nothing.\n17: \n18: | `mutation_status` | Safe interpretation |\n19: |---|---|\n20: | `not_started` | No prompt write began. |\n21: | `accepted` | The agent accepted the prompt. |\n22: | `in_progress` | Work may already be mutating state. |\n23: | `completed` | The turn reached a reported stop reason. |\n24: | `unknown` | Partial mutation cannot be ruled out. |\n25: \n26: Runner never auto-falls back or resends, and never restarts or resumes a session on its own. Read the receipt and decide whether another prompt is appropriate.\n27: \n28: ## Communication loop\n29: \n30: Resolve this Skill's installed directory once and call its scripts by absolute path \u2014 the install\n31: destination may contain spaces, and the user's project is passed only through `--repo` (relative\n32: paths in these references resolve against the Skill, never the project cwd). Progressive\n33: disclosure: open a reference only when the current operation needs it, run the scripts and never read their source, and prefer bounded\n34: receipts (`observe`, `status`, `capture --lines`) over whole-history dumps \u2014 `capture --full` is an\n35: explicit, unbounded request:\n36: \n37: ```bash\n38: SKILL_DIR=\"/absolute/path/to/opencode-kaola-project-runner\"   # the directory containing this file\n39: REPO=\"/abs/canonical/root\"   # canonical Git root, not a linked worktree\n40: SESSION=\"<exact-name>\"   # under Project Runner: <platform>-<CODE>-i<ISSUE>-<purpose>\n41: \"$SKILL_DIR/scripts/runtime-tmux.sh\" preflight --repo \"$REPO\" --session \"$SESSION\"\n42: \"$SKILL_DIR/scripts/runtime-tmux.sh\" start --repo \"$REPO\" --session \"$SESSION\"\n43: \"$SKILL_DIR/scripts/runtime-tmux.sh\" observe --repo \"$REPO\" --session \"$SESSION\"\n44: \"$SKILL_DIR/scripts/runtime-tmux.sh\" capture --repo \"$REPO\" --session \"$SESSION\" --lines 200\n45: ```\n46: \n47: The controlling Agent owns model selection for each `start`. This Skill declares its per-run\n48: presets \u2014 `--tier default` (**DeepSeek V4.1 Flash**: `opencode-go/deepseek-v4.1-flash`, no Runner effort override) only \u2014 and `default` applies whenever nothing more specific was chosen. Pass\n49: another `--tier` when the human or an existing authorization \u2014 the default-authorized Worker pool included \u2014\n50: names that preset; never infer one from code size, failures, elapsed time, or your own assessment.\n51: An undeclared name is refused (`tier-not-declared`), never resolved to `default`.\n52: \n53: An explicit user model choice always wins: pass it with `--model ID`, adding `--effort LEVEL` only\n54: when the user also named an effort. A bare explicit `--model` leaves the runtime's native effort\n55: alone \u2014 never attach a preset's effort to a different model. If the user picks a model ID that\n56: already encodes effort or Fast (such as a `-fast` variant), pass it as-is; the Runner does not\n57: invent extra effort or Fast configuration for it.\n58: \n59: Fast is OFF by default. Pass `--fast on` only on an explicit user request for Fast; this platform's\n60: Fast support: no native Fast toggle; speed-named catalog models such as zhipuai-coding-plan/glm-5.3-flash are explicit model choices, not a Fast switch. Fast and tier are independent selections. When a native fast model\n61: ID is what the user explicitly selected, it counts as the explicit Fast selection \u2014 report the\n62: conflict honestly if it is also passed with `--fast off`.\n63: \n64: `preflight` and `start` report the requested and resolved selection, and `start` what it applied\n65: (`config_application`, `effective_selection`), including unavailable, unsupported, and unknown\n66: outcomes; `observe`/`status` show the agent's `configOptions` `currentValue`. `--resume`/`--continue`\n67: preserve the saved native session model and effort unless the caller supplies `--tier`, `--model`,\n68: or `--effort`; Fast stays a per-run request (off unless explicitly on). All of this is per-run\n69: input and never rewrites global CLI configuration. A mismatch or unreadable actual model remains\n70: evidence for the Agent and does not disable the communication channel.\n71: \n72: `preflight` reports runtime and optional Kaola carrier evidence. Missing Workflow commands,\n73: configuration health, account state, activity hints, or a changed\n74: snapshot do not authorize or block starting the CLI communication channel.\n75: \n76: Use the evidence internally to choose the next communication action. Do not narrate raw holder,\n77: process, snapshot, model, or activity fields in user progress updates; report only visible\n78: task progress, an actual transport failure, or a decision that genuinely needs the user.\n79: \n80: After reading current evidence, the controlling Agent chooses what to send:\n81: \n82: ```bash\n83: \"$SKILL_DIR/scripts/runtime-tmux.sh\" send --repo \"$REPO\" --session \"$SESSION\" --text '<agent-selected prompt>'\n84: \"$SKILL_DIR/scripts/runtime-tmux.sh\" observe --repo \"$REPO\" --session \"$SESSION\"\n85: \"$SKILL_DIR/scripts/runtime-tmux.sh\" capture --repo \"$REPO\" --session \"$SESSION\" --lines 200\n86: ```\n87: \n88: ## Steering a running turn\n89: \n90: No native mid-turn entry has been verified on OpenCode's ACP surface, so a bare\n91: `steer` refuses and writes nothing. That is an unverified capability, not a\n92: proven absence: nothing here says the entry does not exist. The available path\n93: is the composite, which you choose explicitly:\n94: \n95: ```bash\n96: \"$SKILL_DIR/scripts/runtime-tmux.sh\" steer --repo \"$REPO\" --session \"$SESSION\" \\\n97:   --steer-mode interrupt --text '<redirection>'\n98: ```\n99: \n100: It **cancels** the running turn, confirms it stopped, then sends your text as the\n101: next turn on the same session, which keeps the conversation's context. That is\n102: interrupted-then-continued, never injection: work in progress stops and may have\n103: left partial side effects (`side_effects_possible`). An unconfirmed cancel sends\n104: nothing and reports `unknown`. See [references/steering.md](references/steering.md).\n105: \n106: ## Cancel and permissions\n107: \n108: `key --key escape` (or `cancel`) cancels the running turn; there are no other native keys, menus,\n109: or editor replacement. Settle a pending permission `request_id` with\n110: `permit [--request-id ID] --option OPTION_ID` (an option the request offers); omitting `--option`\n111: answers `cancelled`, which denies. Read the resulting output before choosing another action.\n112: \n113: Permission default: Native V2 build full-access default only for fresh managed launches without existing config sources; existing global/project/native config is left intact (including non-permission config). Explicit --permission-mode selects the native build/plan agent via mode and suppresses the default. No auto-permit; remaining requests require an Agent decision..\n114: \n115: When the Agent decides the exact session is finished, end only that owned session:\n116: \n117: ```bash\n118: \"$SKILL_DIR/scripts/runtime-tmux.sh\" stop --repo \"$REPO\" --session \"$SESSION\"\n119: \"$SKILL_DIR/scripts/runtime-tmux.sh\" status --repo \"$REPO\" --session \"$SESSION\"\n120: ```\n121: \n122: Use `--force` only when the Agent explicitly chooses forced containment for this exact owned\n123: session. Never use broad session/process cleanup, and never reconstruct ownership from process\n124: names or fuzzy session matches.\n125: \n126: ## Ending, releasing, and resuming\n127: \n128: The controlling Agent owns every completion judgment; the Runner executes the chosen operation\n129: and reports the true result. These are operation facts, never gates:\n130: \n131: - A finished reply is not a finished task: an `end_turn` event, an idle terminal, or a\n132:   successful `send` receipt never establishes completion.\n133: - `stop` releases the owned runtime (the ACP holder and its agent). It never deletes CLI\n134:   history, session records, work artifacts, or unrelated resources. Judge success by the\n135:   `stop`/`status` result evidence, not by a completed call.\n136: - Before stopping, the Agent may keep available resume facts (platform, canonical repo, any\n137:   reported native session ID, outcome, remaining work) from existing receipts and Workflow\n138:   records. The native session ID is the CLI's own conversation identifier, never the Runner's\n139:   `--session` name. Missing identifiers never block a chosen `stop`.\n140: - Later work resumes by the Agent's choice: `start --resume <native-session-id>`,\n141:   `start --continue` for the platform's latest conversation, or a fresh `start` plus existing\n142:   records where the platform cannot resume.\n143: \n144: ## Optional Kaola Workflow\n145: \n146: Ordinary Workflow-backed work binds `--repo` to the consuming project's canonical Git root and asks this CLI to invoke its installed\n147: `workflow-next`, so that runtime's Workflow creates or recovers the child worktree; this Skill only\n148: transports the exact session. Linked-worktree starts, outer-prepared bundles, and existing-run\n149: recovery are Agent decisions, not transport gates. A session already in a child worktree is advisory: preserve work, then continue,\n150: stop/restart at root, or use another Workflow recovery path. Installation for another runtime alone\n151: does not establish availability here.\n152: \n153: ## Evidence boundary\n154: \n155: - `capture`, events, process facts, permission facts, activity hints, and snapshot changes are\n156:   evidence for the Agent.\n157: - Exact session ownership, platform/repository identity, holder identity, prompt fingerprinting,\n158:   and outbound redaction are transport integrity checks.\n159: - The Runner never classifies evidence into permission to act. The Agent handles every runtime or\n160:   Workflow problem after reading the evidence.\n161: - No invocation implicitly starts `workflow-next`, installs commands, materializes repository files,\n162:   creates a heartbeat, or selects recurring behavior. The Agent may send any of those commands when\n163:   it decides they serve the user's task.\n164: \n165: See [references/platform.md](references/platform.md) for OpenCode launch/observation facts\n166: and [references/acp.md](references/acp.md) for the structured ACP command surface.",
